and the builder copies each span across through `robo_paint_text_pool_copy`, the one door between two pools, which moves bytes between owned arrays rather than round-tripping through a buffer sized to the longest run in the document), which is what makes **clearing the records unable to change a character the display list draws** — 11.2's snapshot property one stage further along, pinned by a case that frees the records and finds the artifact byte-identical and still painting the same pixels; a span counts **code points**, not bytes and not glyph ids (code points are the unit RoboLayout measures in, so a run is measured and painted in the same unit; a glyph id is a fact about a *font*, so a list that stored them would be a list only that font could draw and "does the painter's face agree with the traversal's face?" would be a question nothing could answer — storing characters keeps the display list a statement about the **document** and puts glyph resolution at the drawing step, where RoboGraphics' own bridge says it belongs, leaving the Phase 14 seam already the right shape: this build runs `text run → the font's cmap, per code point, in logical order → paint`, and Phase 14 runs `text run → RoboText shaping → a glyph run → paint`); the `TEXT` record (kind 8, a **drawing** kind) carries the run beside the span — the baseline as an **offset from the run rectangle's top rather than a coordinate** (a single number in one space, so there are not two spaces to keep in agreement), **read through `robo_layout_baseline_offset` and never recomputed** because 10.7 placed it and a painter that derived it would be a second baseline model, and the font inputs **by value** as the measurer's own record, which is what makes "the size it was measured at is the size it is drawn at" a copy rather than an agreement to maintain; a run is a **child in the sequence, not a box** — one record at one depth past the box whose element owns the text node, naming itself and appearing **after that box's `CONTENT`**, with **no `BEGIN`/`END` pair** because a run has nothing to open, nothing to clip for and no children, so a pair would be a boundary a consumer could only skip; the `draw-text` command (kind 5) is the one field on the display model that is not a value, and it names **this list's own** pool; **glyphs reach a surface** — the pass resolves each code point through the **caller's face** and strokes its outline with `robo_gfx_raster_draw_glyph` at the run's own pixel size, from the rectangle's left edge and on the baseline, advancing by the glyph's own device metrics, and a glyph whose metrics will not parse advances by **nothing** and is counted as a miss, which is the measurer's refusal restated (a broken font that produced a narrower run here would pile glyphs on each other in a way that reads as a rendering bug rather than a missing table); the pass **prepares the face's cmap once before the first lookup**, because a `robo_font_face_t` is created without one and `robo_font_face_lookup_glyph` answers `ROBO_NOT_SUPPORTED` for a face whose map has not been parsed — the same door `RoboLayoutTextFont.c` calls — and a face whose map **will not parse** is still not a refusal, since every glyph becomes `.notdef` and `glyphs_missing` names the fact per code point; the pass record grows `texts`, `no_font`, `glyphs`, `glyphs_missing` and `rtl` and the identities move with them (`draws = backgrounds + borders + texts`, `draws = filled + contextual + transparent + empty + no_font`), with a text run **exempt from the zero-area rule** because a run's rectangle is its *advance* box and a glyph's ink can overhang it, and `no_font` on the far side of `draws` from the other three because a caller with no face is a fact about the **call** rather than the document — which is exactly what lets a caller that forgot a face tell its blank frame apart from a textless page; the two dumps each append ` text="…"`, bounded and escaped, the one thing a record's own writer cannot produce because a span is two integers and the characters belong to the pool, so the door that *has* the pool appends them; version **0.6.0** with `text_painting` beside `background_painting` and `border_painting` and `raster` still **0**; and with eight suites over 110 cases, the phase found that a **structural check ordered before the rule it exempts is unreachable** (the builder's text branch sat after the generic "a record's depth is its innermost box's" check, a rule a run fails by construction since a run is owed one level *down* from the box that owns it, so every document with text was refused with that message and the branch beneath it could never run — an ordering, not a rule, and nothing but a document with text could expose it), that a **face is created without a charmap and the drawing layer must prepare it** (without the call the pass drew nothing and reported every code point missing — a rendering-shaped failure with a font-shaped cause), that a **synthetic face can be *nearly* right** (the suite's hand-written format-4 cmap carried one segment too many for two, which shifted `idRangeOffset` onto the wrong word; the parser read a plausible glyph array out of the extra bytes, every lookup resolved past `maxp`'s `numGlyphs`, and the pass reported `INVALID_FONT` while drawing nothing — the two ways a font test passes for the wrong reason, recorded together because the second looks exactly like a rendering bug from outside), and that a **wrapped line is two participants rather than one fragmented run** (RoboLayout measures a text node as a **unit**, so a run wider than its line wraps whole rather than breaking; the case that expected fragments was asserting a later phase's behaviour and now asserts the build's, which is the stronger statement because it pins that the wrap is layout's rather than paint's), and goal **11.7** complete — the **image painting**, the fourth drawing kind and the first whose appearance this module does **not** resolve: an `<img>`'s decoded resource reaches a surface at the rectangle layout gave its element, and the whole release is the cost of a payload that is a **resource** rather than a number — `robo_paint_image_t` holds a **retained** `robo_gfx_image_t` (its identity, its width, height and format) beside the **source rectangle** and the snapshot facts, so a record is still a value the list owns while the resource is refcounted by the runtime's own mechanism instead of copied into a list; the `IMAGE` record (kind 14) is emitted **inside its own element's box, before that box's `CONTENT`**, because an image *is* the element's content where a text run is a child a level below, its **destination is the content box layout resolved** and its source the **whole image** (an image is not re-fitted to a box it was laid out in, and `object-fit` is unregistered); the colour rule **splits** rather than extends — `robo_paint_op_kind_draws` had been the predicate the record validity rule was stated on and eleven releases assumed a drawing *has* a colour, so a new positive list `robo_paint_op_kind_uses_color` names the kinds whose look *is* a colour and `IMAGE` is the first that draws outside it, the alternative (adding `IMAGE` to `draws`) demanding a colour of a record whose appearance is a resource and refusing every image in every document; RoboGraphics gains the one primitive the phase needed and no second pipeline (`robo_gfx_raster_draw_image`, graphics **0.17.0**): nearest-neighbour sampling from the full source into the destination, the source's alpha preserved through the blend the raster already used, and the **raster's own clip** honoured — which is how 11.8's boundary bounds a picture without this module knowing a boundary exists; the missing-image policy is a **counted skip** and never a placeholder (no `src`, never resolved, resource not found, refused scheme and a resource that did not decode all produce **no record** and one `image_skipped`, while a zero-sized destination is a counted `image_empty` rather than a refusal, so `images` stays tellable from "every image failed"); both containers gained a **retain at append and a release at clear/destroy**, written once per file as a static walk both paths call, because a container that released on one path and not the other is a leak in a *failure* path; version **0.10.0** with `image_painting` **1**, `raster` still **0**, and thirteen suite files over **203** cases, the phase finding that a **mem-debug baseline is not a leak test** (comparing the live-block count before a rig with the count after its teardown measures what the harness had already allocated; running the whole build-paint-detach-destroy-paint-teardown cycle **twice** and comparing the two ends measures the module's ownership instead), that **an image's placement does not generalise from a run's** (the order case had to be written for the image's own group — `begin-node … image … content … end-node` — rather than borrowed from 11.6's), and that a **hand-built record needs boxes that are already valid** (the validity rule refuses un-nested boxes before it reads a payload, so a case built on zeroed geometry would have passed every image assertion for the wrong reason); **Next:** **Phase 11.11 — paint invalidation**, with goals **11.8** (clipping), **11.9** (opacity) and **11.10** (hit testing) landed while 11.7 was outstanding and recorded in `PROGRESS.md` — they were implemented on a tree that had no image kind at all, so 11.7 is wired into their records rather than appended after them, the goal now standing in numeric position between 11.6 and 11.8)


The target remains a **complete Chromium-class runtime for RoboticelaOS**, independently implemented, while also exposing **native RoboticelaOS capabilities** and a real PostgreSQL + Prisma application stack.

